tudents need to get ahead? How can graduates be thoughtful,\neffective, purposeful users of this technology, benefitting from its\nvalue rather than becoming its casualties?","position":{"start":{"line":68,"column":1},"end":{"line":68,"column":1}},"key":"yPppCfLGCY"}],"key":"LdaSR4falB"}],"key":"pMVUfnzl3a"}],"key":"Zrj6SeKGgg"},{"type":"caption","children":[{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":73,"column":1},"end":{"line":73,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"abbreviation","title":"In Washington Monthly, April 15, 2026","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Ben Wildavsky","key":"pekRRUnB6T"}],"key":"irmr2hfquP"}],"key":"E1aKtuwdY6"}],"key":"cpmIp4aBo0"}],"key":"DQ4VYDeiI9"}],"class":"dropdown","key":"hMQs3Ox3k9"},{"type":"container","kind":"quote","children":[{"type":"blockquote","children":[{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":77,"column":1},"end":{"line":78,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"There’s still that to build, and I think that would be one of the most consequential\ninventions humanity will ever invent.","position":{"start":{"line":77,"column":1},"end":{"line":77,"column":1}},"key":"ostPE5ifSe"}],"key":"HXQDWANCaZ"},{"type":"container","kind":"quote","children":[{"type":"blockquote","position":{"start":{"line":79,"column":1},"end":{"line":80,"column":1}},"children":[],"key":"qUdlPZxNbd"},{"type":"caption","children":[{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":79,"column":1},"end":{"line":80,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"abbreviation","title":"From, Interview with the 2024 chemistry laureate Demis Hassabis, recorded on\n6 December 2024 during Nobel Week in Stockholm, Sweden.","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Demis Hassabis","key":"jYpytfxUQM"}],"key":"nCttT5ShZe"}],"key":"MsFb9Foby6"}],"key":"clNTiW7HS5"}],"key":"BLI9ru8XGq"}],"key":"vKt0icAo2x"}],"class":"pull-quote","key":"ead04bsk3J"},{"type":"admonition","kind":"note","children":[{"type":"admonitionTitle","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Full quote","position":{"start":{"line":83,"column":1},"end":{"line":83,"column":1}},"key":"lkNvfEykRC"}],"key":"A7v3lMX9zs"},{"type":"container","kind":"quote","children":[{"type":"blockquote","position":{"start":{"line":85,"column":1},"end":{"line":96,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":85,"column":1},"end":{"line":93,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"emphasis","position":{"start":{"line":85,"column":1},"end":{"line":85,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"My original ambition in AI is to build what we sometimes call\nartificial general intelligence, which is a system that can exhibit\nall the cognitive capabilities that humans can. Then we know we have\na truly general system at that point, a kind of Turing\nmachine. There’s still that to build, and I think that would be one\nof the most consequential inventions humanity will ever invent. I\nguess that was my original goal when I got into AI, 30 plus years\nago and that’s still my goal now. We still have a lot of work to do\nto get there.","position":{"start":{"line":85,"column":1},"end":{"line":85,"column":1}},"key":"Ws8LYovEH3"}],"key":"OQ4wBlI7RP"}],"key":"Fod1c8y7ge"}],"key":"jAe5ofEPq4"},{"type":"caption","children":[{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":95,"column":1},"end":{"line":96,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"abbreviation","title":"From, Interview with the 2024 chemistry laureate Demis Hassabis, recorded on\n6 December 2024 during Nobel Week in Stockholm, Sweden.","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Demis Hassabis","key":"eYPV0vfLy5"}],"key":"h1MzJCJH0Z"}],"key":"oQ3pjjTcy7"}],"key":"b1ouEkGRRX"}],"key":"SmFPwJnf2O"}],"class":"dropdown","key":"QEvimSZ0w1"},{"type":"container","kind":"quote","children":[{"type":"blockquote","children":[{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":100,"column":1},"end":{"line":100,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"... it can be used for good, which of course, ... but it can also be repurposed by bad actors for harmful ends.","position":{"start":{"line":100,"column":1},"end":{"line":100,"column":1}},"key":"yrDuOnuV4g"}],"key":"qs6iuaHrbq"},{"type":"container","kind":"quote","children":[{"type":"blockquote","position":{"start":{"line":101,"column":1},"end":{"line":103,"column":1}},"children":[],"key":"nEdnuvFLDM"},{"type":"caption","children":[{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":102,"column":1},"end":{"line":103,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"abbreviation","title":"From, Interview with the 2024 chemistry laureate Demis Hassabis, recorded on\n6 December 2024 during Nobel Week in Stockholm, Sweden.","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Demis Hassabis","key":"sPOZPIcCNP"}],"key":"RbAPQbp1dH"}],"key":"qOKqPbLlkY"}],"key":"Urd2O0Rmpm"}],"key":"QAjG2Uwg6T"}],"key":"hhxh6awtgZ"}],"class":"pull-quote","key":"liHHjg5jFj"},{"type":"admonition","kind":"note","children":[{"type":"admonitionTitle","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Full quote","position":{"start":{"line":106,"column":1},"end":{"line":106,"column":1}},"key":"O5jFZRRM1M"}],"key":"DM9fgJ3eus"},{"type":"container","kind":"quote","children":[{"type":"blockquote","position":{"start":{"line":108,"column":1},"end":{"line":124,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":108,"column":1},"end":{"line":121,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"emphasis","position":{"start":{"line":108,"column":1},"end":{"line":108,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"I think there are two concerns on AI for me. One is, these are\ngeneral purpose systems and, like any powerful general purpose\ntechnology, it can be used for good, which of course, things like\nmedicine and climate and help us with some of humanity’s greatest\nchallenges, but it can also be repurposed by bad actors for\nharmfulends. Thetechnology itself is kind of neutral. It depends on\nhow we as scientists and humanity and society decide are going to\nuse it and deploy it. I worry about the bad actors and how they may\naccess the same technologies for harmful ends. So that’s one\nthing. The second thing is, as the systems become more and more\npowerful themselves, more autonomous, more agent like, can we\nunderstand and keep control of those systems, make sure they have\nthe right goals and values and how do we ensure that the guardrails\naround how those systems work are robust enough?","position":{"start":{"line":108,"column":1},"end":{"line":108,"column":1}},"key":"BUCxPnbNge"}],"key":"e5I0L4Mi7Z"}],"key":"auNi74q35R"}],"key":"ALhIUGhyqP"},{"type":"caption","children":[{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":123,"column":1},"end":{"line":124,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"abbreviation","title":"From, Interview with the 2024 chemistry laureate Demis Hassabis, recorded on\n6 December 2024 during Nobel Week in Stockholm, Sweden.","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Demis Hassabis","key":"XAoXO9nmmm"}],"key":"qVrGDPU1Ls"}],"key":"ckiO6hJIM6"}],"key":"mJFCwD3uk6"}],"key":"YTwpbhlGku"}],"class":"dropdown","key":"Hzlhm67rSW"},{"type":"container","kind":"quote","children":[{"type":"blockquote","children":[{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":128,"column":1},"end":{"line":128,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"The United States is at a pivotal moment.","position":{"start":{"line":128,"column":1},"end":{"line":128,"column":1}},"key":"FyTYNh34UE"}],"key":"XPQyJexWoG"},{"type":"container","kind":"quote","children":[{"type":"blockquote","position":{"start":{"line":129,"column":1},"end":{"line":131,"column":1}},"children":[],"key":"L4xkxo2bAj"},{"type":"caption","children":[{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":130,"column":1},"end":{"line":131,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"abbreviation","title":"The Role of Applied Mathematics in\na New Era of Artificial Intelligence, February 2026","children":[{"type":"text","value":"SIAM AI Task Force Report","key":"Pzjf0hrdhz"}],"key":"eYD061qXY8"}],"key":"yRfYPz0mJT"}],"key":"OOTyn8FpTo"}],"key":"xGhS2fNAts"}],"key":"G5TP3LhLIO"}],"class":"pull-quote","key":"A5AG8wxuRf"},{"type":"admonition","kind":"note","children":[{"type":"admonitionTitle","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Full quote","position":{"start":{"line":134,"column":1},"end":{"line":134,"column":1}},"key":"WhZuEnOZmR"}],"key":"bemxD3E0ds"},{"type":"container","kind":"quote","children":[{"type":"blockquote","position":{"start":{"line":136,"column":1},"end":{"line":156,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":136,"column":1},"end":{"line":140,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"emphasis","position":{"start":{"line":136,"column":1},"end":{"line":136,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"The United States is at a pivotal moment. Multi-billion-dollar\nfederal AI investments, rapid commercial deployment, and\nuniversity-wide AI initiatives are collectively setting the\ntrajectory of the nation’s scientific, economic, and security\nlandscape for decades to come.","position":{"start":{"line":136,"column":1},"end":{"line":136,"column":1}},"key":"Zl25dgaSTf"}],"key":"AAIOh3UiKe"}],"key":"ubKpq91Cqg"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":142,"column":1},"end":{"line":153,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"emphasis","position":{"start":{"line":142,"column":1},"end":{"line":142,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"At the same time: AI systems are being deployed faster than our\nability to understand, verify, certify, or govern them.  Federal\nagencies are launching large-scale AI programs with expectations for\nnear-term impact, often without proportional investment in\nmathematical foundations needed to understand, evaluate and\nsystematically improve AI systems. Universities are shifting hiring,\ncurricula, and research infrastructure around AI, often without\ncommensurate investment in applied mathematics, with lasting\nworkforce consequences.  Global industrial leadership is shifting\nfrom those with the most data to those with the mathematical insight\nto transform raw AI potential into reliable, efficient, scalable,\nand sovereign economic power.","position":{"start":{"line":142,"column":1},"end":{"line":142,"column":1}},"key":"PwRj1cI9cg"}],"key":"JEz4Z4j8aD"}],"key":"Mu5BIjPukf"}],"key":"kHXuny6jtn"},{"type":"caption","children":[{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":155,"column":1},"end":{"line":156,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"abbreviation","title":"The Role of Applied Mathematics in\na New Era of Artificial Intelligence, February 2026","children":[{"type":"text","value":"SIAM AI Task Force Report","key":"gFLZBDjDih"}],"key":"XRH28LOSmc"}],"key":"HgoENoJ89O"}],"key":"XXUiTvJzv8"}],"key":"LuOeyJezgI"}],"class":"dropdown","key":"iSq8D6JWVI"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"position":{"start":{"line":159,"column":1},"end":{"line":159,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Questions","position":{"start":{"line":159,"column":1},"end":{"line":159,"column":1}},"key":"nPzak6kH0P"}],"identifier":"questions","label":"Questions","html_id":"questions","implicit":true,"key":"tRPjnZIsh4"},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"spread":false,"position":{"start":{"line":161,"column":1},"end":{"line":163,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"listItem","spread":true,"position":{"start":{"line":161,"column":1},"end":{"line":161,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"[] Is AI a villain?","position":{"start":{"line":161,"column":1},"end":{"line":161,"column":1}},"key":"ob0PX03Xln"}],"key":"ugJKJLkTWJ"}],"key":"eORs1d6wD9"},{"type":"listItem","spread":true,"position":{"start":{"line":162,"column":1},"end":{"line":163,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"[] or a saint?","position":{"start":{"line":162,"column":1},"end":{"line":162,"column":1}},"key":"TTgM9vJzsk"}],"key":"Jm0UxXN0c5"}],"key":"zPyKCI37LZ"}],"key":"cx9DaiUwdK"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":164,"column":1},"end":{"line":166,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"I believe you get the idea - there are many reasoned ideas out there, but even more opinions. As for myself, I have no\ninterest whatsoever in opinions. They muddy the waters and distract from the serious issues. It reminds me of something\nTim Minchun once said:","position":{"start":{"line":164,"column":1},"end":{"line":164,"column":1}},"key":"q3C31ilCb5"}],"key":"WL95reIt0p"},{"type":"container","kind":"quote","children":[{"type":"blockquote","position":{"start":{"line":168,"column":1},"end":{"line":173,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":168,"column":1},"end":{"line":171,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"emphasis","position":{"start":{"line":168,"column":1},"end":{"line":168,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Be hard on your opinions. We must hink critically and not just about\nthe ideas of others, be hard on your believes, take them out onto\nthe verandah and hit them with a cricket bat. Be intellectually\nrigorous, identify your biases, your prejudices, your priviledges.","position":{"start":{"line":168,"column":1},"end":{"line":168,"column":1}},"key":"nzwzelsE50"}],"key":"hgdMjqA6v5"}],"key":"uDa312Oovd"}],"key":"p97krOdvrJ"},{"type":"caption","children":[{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":173,"column":1},"end":{"line":173,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"abbreviation","title":"\"9 Life Lessons\" speech delivered to graduates at the University of Western Australia.","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Tim Minchin","key":"Csxh22g7XH"}],"key":"y4XYHZxdcq"}],"key":"V3imybUeDL"}],"key":"udBaONvYSz"}],"key":"BnweOo6Fhl"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":175,"column":1},"end":{"line":175,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Of course this applies to me as well.","position":{"start":{"line":175,"column":1},"end":{"line":175,"column":1}},"key":"ygOkkDfNwd"}],"key":"TRlKMGFXzD"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":177,"column":1},"end":{"line":182,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"But, if the opinions are from tech leaders, they are consequencial. Some of these leaders are incredibly successfull\nand highly influential. Their opinions are consequential. What should our responce be as members of societies with value\nsystems that we hold dear? Doing nothing, risks being overwhelmed by a tsunami of sorts. And if we think a tsunami is\nnot all good, how do we stop it? Or rather, direct it in a direction of our choosing where it can do the most good.\nEven if it is hard to direct a tsunami, it is important to realise that we ordinary people collectively have enormous\npower. Quoting Demis Hassibis again,","position":{"start":{"line":177,"column":1},"end":{"line":177,"column":1}},"key":"iyzQ7T0wxx"}],"key":"NgxHyOTw5I"},{"type":"container","kind":"quote","children":[{"type":"blockquote","position":{"start":{"line":184,"column":1},"end":{"line":194,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":184,"column":1},"end":{"line":191,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"emphasis","position":{"start":{"line":184,"column":1},"end":{"line":184,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Human intelligence always astounds me, and I don’t think we think\nabout this enough. It’s created modern civilisation around\nus. Sometimes when I’m flying over to the US for a business trip or\nsomething on a 747, I sometimes look out the window and think, “how\nhave we as humanity manage this with our sort of primate brains?” It\nseems incredible to me, and I don’t think people stop and think how\nmagical that really is. So really the power of intelligence, human\nintelligence, has created the wonders of modern civilisation.","position":{"start":{"line":184,"column":1},"end":{"line":184,"column":1}},"key":"FIHX5H1I2u"}],"key":"h5Dmb0QJx7"}],"key":"jq5HqRo2ZC"}],"key":"Q38EY2nkGe"},{"type":"caption","children":[{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":193,"column":1},"end":{"line":194,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"abbreviation","title":"From, Interview with the 2024 chemistry laureate Demis Hassabis, recorded on\n6 December 2024 during Nobel Week in Stockholm, Sweden.","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Demis Hassabis","key":"SW6VTyAQvV"}],"key":"RQcUHvjRcc"}],"key":"GYMANXUAbb"}],"key":"yEDLm5VJnB"}],"key":"mIbjZn0RQN"},{"type":"container","kind":"quote","children":[{"type":"blockquote","children":[{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":198,"column":1},"end":{"line":198,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"We must never surrender our intellectual autonomy.","position":{"start":{"line":198,"column":1},"end":{"line":198,"column":1}},"key":"S73vGBBVxy"}],"key":"ZOXitDMYOu"}],"key":"gIN6K91033"}],"class":"pull-quote","key":"XXfrLWk31q"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":201,"column":1},"end":{"line":208,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"The thing is, we as humans have amazing intelligence with almost\nunlimited potential. We might even be able to build machines smarter\nthan ourselves! We must never surrender our intillectual autonomy. But\nthe question remains, how do we respond to the AI revolution? One\nanswer is to use our wondrous intellect to equip ourselves to\nparticipate in a wide, informed debate of where we want to go, and how\nto get there. ","position":{"start":{"line":201,"column":1},"end":{"line":201,"column":1}},"key":"zERs32Qg7i"},{"type":"emphasis","position":{"start":{"line":201,"column":1},"end":{"line":201,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Machines that Think","position":{"start":{"line":201,"column":1},"end":{"line":201,"column":1}},"key":"HwgYRqNuPY"}],"key":"SjltR4Flee"},{"type":"text","value":" is an attempt to provide some of\nthat equipment.","position":{"start":{"line":201,"column":1},"end":{"line":201,"column":1}},"key":"UPFjbAsVM5"}],"key":"CLC5kYPQXr"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":210,"column":1},"end":{"line":220,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"With ","position":{"start":{"line":210,"column":1},"end":{"line":210,"column":1}},"key":"xExEVtZRMW"},{"type":"abbreviation","title":"Machines that Think","children":[{"type":"text","value":"MtT","key":"LK4PcJiGMS"}],"key":"aqaqtpgTYg"},{"type":"text","value":" Inga managed to write a book\nthat is both informative and entertaining. She writes in a lively,\nengaging style and her passion for AI shines through the whole\nbook. Most of her explanations of technical issues are brilliant. Yes,\nshe does not shy away from technicalities when it is necessary to\nfollow the broader argument. One exception is her discussion of\nGodel’s incompleteness theorem ","position":{"start":{"line":210,"column":1},"end":{"line":210,"column":1}},"key":"TXY3GaUd03"},{"type":"citeGroup","kind":"parenthetical","position":{"start":{"line":210,"column":1},"end":{"line":210,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"cite","identifier":"nagel1958godel","label":"nagel1958godel","kind":"parenthetical","position":{"start":{"line":210,"column":432},"end":{"line":210,"column":447}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Nagel & Newman, 1958","key":"F5z2p6DpnM"}],"enumerator":"1","key":"BkZCmuIahB"}],"key":"fOsw6ZzktB"},{"type":"text","value":" which I found\npretty hard going.  For the intrepid who wants to know more, Natalie\nWolchover provides ","position":{"start":{"line":210,"column":1},"end":{"line":210,"column":1}},"key":"hq7RRCNy9w"},{"type":"link","url":"https://www.quantamagazine.org/how-godels-proof-works-20200714/","position":{"start":{"line":210,"column":1},"end":{"line":210,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"an accessible\noverview","position":{"start":{"line":210,"column":1},"end":{"line":210,"column":1}},"key":"fVrzvcxZQ9"}],"urlSource":"https://www.quantamagazine.org/how-godels-proof-works-20200714/","key":"Wq2KW4gIXo"},{"type":"text","value":",\nbased on ","position":{"start":{"line":210,"column":1},"end":{"line":210,"column":1}},"key":"PegW8tyzXt"},{"ty